"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-31T20:25:36-08:00","id":1364460,"title":"","content":"Man, stuff's been shitty lately.<br /> <br /> However, in December, we adopted two cats from a shelter. We'd been on the fence for a while about a dog (my wife was for) or a cat (I've had both, and didn't like the dogs) and allergy testing on our son revealed a dog allergy so we went with the felines. They have been adapting to the household and man. I had a cat when I was a kid, from a kitten, just saved it from being drowned and took care of it, had it declawed (we know now that's a no-no) and it was lazy as fuck. If it was not running away from everyone, it was sleeping. 17 years or so, it lived, and it was almost always like that. So I was expecting, hey, low impact animals, get him used to them, no big deal. And one of them is sort of like that. He likes to play, but he's very skittish and will generally run away from folks unless they're coming to him with food. The other is like he's joined <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pqb9pBJweVU\" target=\"_blank\">the cult of crossfit</a>. When he's not sleeping, he's finding one of us (usually me or my wife) and clawing us to get us to play. We made it clear the claw = no play, so now he just taps us with his paw. But he wants to play. Rigorously. Like he wants a Rocky-style montage of exercise ALL THE TIME. He pants at the end of it, which seems to be the time he realizes that, yeah, it was good. But anything else and he's like 'My BPM dropped, I gotta get fit bro, you gotta help me bro, exercise with me bro.' And so I play with him and get him to do great jumps in the air and run up and down the stairs and jump onto and off of the couch and man this is a hella active cat. They're both great but it goes against a lot about what I know regarding cats.<br /> <br /> Grown a lot, they're going to be one year old next month and I think that's when most of 